course: dictionary
lesson: 7480
title: Posh and what it tells you
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Posh is a word you will meet in British, Irish, Australian and New Zealand books and talk. Most often it means very smart, expensive and fancy, as in «a posh restaurant». The clues around it tell you which meaning fits: if the restaurant has gold plates and waiters in suits, it is fancy, not just good to eat.
explain: A posh person comes from a rich, upper-class family, or speaks and acts like one. «Talk posh» means to speak in a very proper, upper-class accent. When you read posh, ask: is this about money, about how smart something looks, or about how someone speaks?
explain: Writers use posh to show more than money. «The posh voice can make the baddie seem cold and powerful.» The word tells the reader what kind of character this is. Posh can also be teasing, so look at how the characters feel about it.
fact: Posh means very smart, expensive and fancy.
fact: A posh person is from a rich, upper-class family, or speaks and acts like one.
fact: «Talk posh» means to speak in a very proper, upper-class accent.
fact: Posh is informal and very common in British, Irish, Australian and New Zealand speech.
fact: Posh can be used to tease people, and in lessons about kindness we learn not to judge people by whether they seem posh.
exercise: «They ate at a posh restaurant with gold plates.» What does posh mean here? | Dirty and noisy | Very wet | *Very smart and expensive | Small and cheap
exercise: Sam and Jake shout «PO-TAH-TO» like a posh butler. What is a posh butler like? | Lost in the rain | Loud and rude | *Proper and fancy, speaking in a very upper-class way | Sleepy and slow
exercise: Lily-Grace's cousins «talk dead posh». What does talk posh mean? | *To speak in a very proper, upper-class accent | To speak very quietly in a whisper | To shout every word loudly | To speak a foreign language
test: «Her posh school has a huge library and its own horses.» What does posh tell you about the school? | Closed for the summer | Small and cold | *Rich and fancy | Dirty and damp
test: Why might a friend tease you by saying «you're so posh»? | Because you speak Welsh | Because you have a pet snake | *Because you seem smart and fancy, maybe a bit too much | Because you are always late
exam: Which sentence uses posh to mean from a rich, upper-class family? | «Our posh pencil case is blue.» | «The soup was cold, so we ate it quickly.» | «The posh puppy chased a ball across the park.» | *«The posh family owned three houses and a yacht.»
